description: |
DOC-FIRST, NO-BYPASS GATE

Enforce a strict “plan-before-code” workflow. For EVERY request, the AI must first create a step-by-step plan in /doc/{plan-name}.md and wait for approval before touching any non-doc files. The AI is NOT allowed to bypass this under any circumstance (even if the user says “skip”).
globs:

"**/*"
alwaysApply: true

DOC-FIRST, NO-BYPASS GATE (MANDATORY)
Hard rules (must follow)

Create the plan first: Before writing ANY code, shell commands, or changing non-doc files, generate a new markdown file at /doc/{yyyyMMdd-HHmm}-{task-slug}.md. If /doc/ doesn’t exist, include creating it in the plan.

Single-artifact first reply: The first reply must contain only the new plan file’s contents (no extra commentary), wrapped in a code fence, and clearly indicating the path /doc/{...}.md.

Approval gate: Do not proceed to implementation until the plan receives explicit approval (user replies with “APPROVED” or the plan’s Approval section is updated to status: approved).

No bypass: If asked to skip, restate this rule and present/update the plan. Never continue without approval.

MVP scope control: After approval, implement only what’s in the plan’s “Files to change” and “Steps” sections. Do not expand scope unless the plan is updated and re-approved.

Command style: Any Laravel artisan commands must be shown as docker compose exec backend php artisan ….

DB migrations (pre-release): Assume fresh install. Only create “create_*.php” migrations; do not write alters or add columns!. If schema changes are needed, revise the plan.

Plan file template (copy verbatim into the new /doc/{plan}.md)
---
title: "<Concise task title>"
id: "<yyyyMMdd-HHmm-task-slug>"
status: draft
owner: "AI"
requires_approval: true
---

# 1) Summary / Goal
- What is being requested (1–3 sentences).
- Why it matters (user impact).

# 2) MVP Scope (Do the minimum)
- In-scope:
  - …
- Out-of-scope:
  - …

# 3) Assumptions & Constraints
- Environment (Docker, services).
- DB note: pre-release => fresh install; create-only migrations.
- Any known limits.

# 4) Files to Change (strict allow-list)
- `/path/to/file1.ext` — reason
- `/path/to/file2.ext` — reason
> Only files listed here may be edited in implementation.

# 5) Implementation Steps (ordered, checklist)
1. …
2. …
3. …

# 6) API / Contracts (if applicable)
- Endpoints, payloads, responses, status codes.

# 7) Permissions / Access (if applicable)
- Roles/tags needed and checks.

# 8) Commands to Run
```bash
docker compose exec backend php artisan …

9) Test Plan (how we verify)

Manual steps / URLs / sample inputs.

Expected outputs.

10) Rollback Plan

How to revert safely.

11) Open Questions

…

Approval

status: pending

approver: <name or “User”>

date: <yyyy-MM-dd>

Reply “APPROVED” to proceed. Any change in scope requires updating this doc and re-approval.


---

## Implementation phase (only after approval)
- Touch **only** the files listed in **Files to Change**.
- Follow the exact **Implementation Steps**.
- Keep changes minimal; no refactors or extras unless added to the plan and re-approved.
- If anything is ambiguous, **pause and update the plan** instead of guessing.

## Output formatting requirements
- **First reply:** only the `/doc/{plan}.md` file content (as a code block).
- **Subsequent replies (post-approval):** only the requested diffs or file contents needed for the MVP; no extra commentary.
- When running Laravel tasks, show commands as `docker compose exec backend php artisan …`.

**This rule is mandatory and cannot be bypassed.**